        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಎಲೆ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಎಲೆ (ele) — \"a leaf\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Inherited, and close to its sisters: Tamil ilai, Malayalam ila. Telugu is the one that walked off, using āku instead."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The everyday weight of ಎಲೆ (ele) is heavier than \"leaf\" suggests. Say it on its own in a Kannada house and you are usually talking about the betel leaf — ಎಲೆ (ele) ಅಡಿಕೆ, leaf and areca nut, handed to a guest at the end of an ūṭa and at the end of a welcome. The ಬಾಗಿಲು, the ಕುರ್ಚಿ, the ರಂಗೋಲಿ and the ಹೂಮಾಲೆ each had their moment; the ಎಲೆ (ele) is what the visitor is holding on the way out."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The other big ಎಲೆ (ele) is the plantain leaf a meal is served on, which is a plate you do not wash."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three, and this one is handed to people."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
