        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಬೇರು",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಬೇರು (bēru) — \"a root\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tamil says vēr, Malayalam says vēr, Telugu says vēru — and Kannada says ಬೇರು (bēru). That opening b against everybody else's v is the trade you have watched at the ಬಾಗಿಲು and again in ಬರೆ, and here it is once more with the letters lined up so plainly that you can hear the law rather than be told about it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two sound laws now separate Kannada from its sisters, and both of them are visible in a single chapter: the p that became h in ಹಣ್ಣು and ಹೂವು, and the v that became b in this word."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಬೇರು (bēru) also does the figurative work English gives roots: where a family comes from, what a word descends from. Both languages dug the same hole."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four, and the lowest one shows the law."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
