        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಬೀಜ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಬೀಜ (bīja) — \"a seed\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four inherited words, and then this one, straight out of Sanskrit and unaltered. The chapter closes on a borrowing, which is a fair picture of the language: the tree is Dravidian from the ಬೇರು (bēru) up, and the word for what starts it came from elsewhere."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kannada kept its own word for the act, though. ಬಿತ್ತು (bittu) is to sow, and it is as native as ಮರ (mara). So a ರೈತ sows with an inherited word and names what he is sowing with a borrowed one, in the same breath and without noticing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಬೀಜ (bīja) also does the abstract job — the seed of a quarrel, the seed of an idea — in Kannada exactly as in English, and it was doing it in Sanskrit long before either."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five: ಮರ (mara), ಕೊಂಬೆ (kombe), ಎಲೆ (ele), ಬೇರು (bēru), ಬೀಜ (bīja). A tree from the ground up, and the thing it began as."
            }
          ]
        },
